Keep .env and .env.example in sync

Usage: evnx sync [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --direction <DIRECTION>
          Which file to fill: forward (the default) adds to the template, reverse adds to `.env`.
          
          With only a template (a fresh clone), reverse creates `.env` from it.
          
          Leaving it out also decides how far `--check` looks. Without it the check is two-way, for a developer's own pair: a name only the template has is drift as well. Naming a direction makes the check one-way — only names the target lacks — which is how CI and the pre-commit hook ask: `evnx sync --check --direction forward`.

          Possible values:
          - forward: Sync .env → .env.example (add new vars to template)
          - reverse: Sync .env.example → .env (add missing vars to local env)

      --to <TO>
          The file to fill: `example` is `--direction forward`, `env` is `--direction reverse`.
          
          Another spelling of `--direction`; give one or the other.

          Possible values:
          - example: Add the names `.env` has and the template lacks to the template (forward)
          - env:     Add the names the template has and `.env` lacks to `.env` (reverse)

  -n, --dry-run
          Show what would change without writing anything

      --check
          Report whether the files are in step through the exit code.
          
          Implies --dry-run: nothing is ever written. This is the CI gate.
          
            0  in sync
            1  out of sync — the message names the command that fixes each side
            2  no verdict — a file is missing or will not parse, the config is
               wrong, or (forward) .env is empty beside a populated template
          
          Without --direction it looks both ways: names the template lacks, and
          names only the template has. With --direction it looks one way, as CI
          and the pre-commit hook should:
          
            evnx sync --check --direction forward
          
          With no .env, the template is checked against the required `[vars]` in
          .evnx.toml. The three codes follow `diff` and `grep`. Plain --dry-run
          previews and always exits 0.

      --skip-without-env
          With no `.env` and nothing else to compare, skip (exit 0) and say so.
          
          For pre-commit hooks: a fresh clone has a template and no `.env`, and a hook that exits 2 there blocks every new contributor's first commit. A spec (`[vars]` in `.evnx.toml`) is still checked. Forward only.
          
          Not for CI: without it, `--check` with nothing to compare exits 2, so a gate cannot pass on a check that never ran.

      --template-config <PATH>
          A JSON file of placeholder rules: `patterns`, `default`, `allow_actual`.
          
          An unknown key is an error naming the accepted ones, so a misspelt setting cannot be silently off.

      --prune
          Remove from the template the names that no environment file has.
          
          Compares the template with `.env` and every `.env.<NAME>`, lists what
          goes, and asks. A name declared in `.evnx.toml [vars]` is kept. Only the
          template is written (git holds its history; no `.bak`). Refused when no
          environment file exists or all are empty.
          
          Works on its own: it cannot be combined with the options that pick
          one environment file or another mode (env, env-name, direction, check,
          format, json, to, skip-without-env, template-config,
          naming-policy). Use --example for another template and --dry-run to
          preview.
          
            0  pruned, nothing to prune, or a dry run
            2  refused (no terminal without --yes, no environment file, a file it
               cannot read, changed while running)
            4  you answered no

      --naming-policy <NAMING_POLICY>
          What to do with a name that is not UPPER_SNAKE_CASE.
          
          Defaults to `[sync] naming_policy` in .evnx.toml, else `warn`. `error` stops the run with no verdict (exit 2), in every mode.

          Possible values:
          - warn:   Warn but continue (default)
          - error:  Treat non-standard names as errors
          - ignore: Ignore naming conventions entirely

      --no-color
          Disable colored output

  -q, --quiet
          Print nothing on success. Findings, warnings, errors and requested data still print

  -v, --verbose
          Enable verbose output

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Files:
      --env <FILE>
          The environment file to sync from (forward) or into (reverse). Defaults to `.env`, or `[defaults] env_name` from `.evnx.toml`

      --env-name <NAME>
          Use `.env.<NAME>` instead of `.env`

      --example <FILE>
          The template to sync into (forward) or from (reverse). Defaults to `.env.example`, or `[defaults] example`

Output:
      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format, in every mode.
          
          `json` prints the `--check` report on stdout and nothing else. A run that writes (or, with `--dry-run`, would write) adds an `added` array: the names added to the target, sorted. A run that would stop to ask needs `--yes` or `--dry-run`.
          
          [default: pretty]
          [possible values: pretty, json]

      --json
          Same as --format json

Confirmation:
  -y, --yes
          Skip the prompts and add placeholder values.
          
          Without a terminal, or with CI set, sync does this anyway and says so. With `--prune` it also confirms the removal.

Examples:
  evnx sync --check --direction forward   CI gate: does the template have everything .env has?
  evnx sync --prune --dry-run             preview names in the template that no env file has
  evnx sync --prune --yes                 remove them, without asking

Exit codes:
    0  Ran, nothing to report
    1  Ran, and found something: findings, problems, a difference, not in sync
    2  Could not run: usage, config, I/O, or refused before any change
    4  Declined: answered no, or a confirmation was needed with no terminal and no --yes
  141  Output closed early (broken pipe)

Full guide: https://docs.evnx.dev/cli/commands/sync
